  "question": {
    "text": "The shortest distance between line $y-x=1$ and curve $x=y^2$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "sGR29",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{8}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "ftGLN",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{8}{3 \\sqrt{2}}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ATD4f",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "yqTOv",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$$\nP=\\left(y^2, y\\right)\n$$\nPerpendicular distance from $P$ to $x-y+1=0$ is $\\frac{\\left|y^2-y+1\\right|}{\\sqrt{2}}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\mathrm{y}^2-\\mathrm{y}+1>0 \\forall \\mathrm{y} \\in \\mathrm{R} \\\\\n& \\therefore \\text { Coefficient } \\mathrm{y}^2>0 \\\\\n& \\therefore \\text { Min value }=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left(\\frac{4 \\mathrm{ac}-\\mathrm{b}^2}{4 \\mathrm{a}}\\right)=\\frac{3}{4 \\sqrt{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
